    "body": "For twenty-seven years One Piece was the show that never stopped. It's stopping. Episode 1180 airs September 27, closes the first cour of the Elbaf arc, and then the anime goes dark until sometime in 2027.\n\n## The schedule\nComicBook.com laid out the full Elbaf calendar and CBR called it the end of an era, which is only slightly dramatic. The arc that started this spring runs through 1180, then breaks with no announced return date. The manga is on the same rhythm: Chapter 1194 also lands September 27 after its own short pause.\n\n## The model\nToei has moved One Piece to roughly 26 episodes a year in seasonal blocks, down from the 40-to-50 weekly grind that ran since 1999. The Egghead break was the first time the show did it; Elbaf confirms it's the structure now. The tradeoff is visible on screen: the Elbaf episodes look like a film, and that's because nobody is drawing them the week before they air.\n\n## Why it matters to you\nIf you're 25 to 45, you grew up with One Piece as background radiation, always on, always six hundred episodes ahead of you. It's now a seasonal show, like everything else. That makes it easier to catch up, easier to get your friends in, and harder to take for granted. Watch 1180 live. Then you've got a few months.\n\n## Sources\n- ComicBook.com, One Piece confirms the full Elbaph schedule for 2026: https://comicbook.com/anime/news/one-piece-confirms-the-full-elbaph-schedule-for-2026/\n- CBR, September 2026 is officially the end of an era for One Piece: https://www.cbr.com/one-piece-official-end-of-an-era-september-2026/\n- GamesRadar+, One Piece Elbaph arc release schedule: https://www.gamesradar.com/entertainment/anime-shows/one-piece-elbaph-arc-release-date-time-schedule-crunchyroll-netflix/\n"
